---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Dispatch from '../../components/Dispatch.astro';
import { SITE } from '../../data/site.js';
---

<Base
  title="Pool Pump Humming But Won't Start? What It Means"
  description="A pool pump that hums but won't start is usually a failed start capacitor — one of the cheapest pool repairs there is. Call (512) 764-9867."
>
  <h1>Pool pump humming but not starting: what it means and what it costs</h1>

  <p>
    A pool pump that hums but won't start is, more often than not, a failed start capacitor —
    one of the cheapest and fastest repairs in pool service, not a sign the motor is dead. The
    quick way to tell the difference: with power off at the breaker, spin the motor shaft by
    hand. If it spins freely, the capacitor is the likely culprit. If it's gritty, stiff, or
    won't turn, you're looking at a bearing or seal problem instead.
  </p>

  <Dispatch problem="Pump humming but not starting" area={SITE.serviceArea} />

  <h2>Why a capacitor failure sounds like this</h2>
  <p>
    The start capacitor gives the motor the extra torque it needs to begin spinning from a dead
    stop. When it fails, the motor still receives power — hence the hum — but can't generate
    enough starting force to actually turn, especially on single-speed motors, which rely
    heavily on the capacitor for that initial kick. Once it's running, a motor doesn't need the
    same push, which is why some pumps with a weak capacitor will start fine if you help the
    shaft spin by hand (not something to try without a technician present).
  </p>

  <h2>The shaft-spin test, safely</h2>
  <ol class="steps">
    <li>
      <strong>Kill power at the breaker first.</strong> Not the pump's on/off switch — the
      breaker. This is a non-negotiable safety step before touching anything at the motor.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Locate the motor shaft</strong> at the back of the motor housing, usually with a
      small slot for a screwdriver.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Turn it by hand.</strong> Free and smooth rotation with only a hum under power
      points to the capacitor. Gritty, resistant, or seized rotation points to bearings or a
      worn shaft seal instead.
    </li>
    <li>
      If you're not comfortable doing this yourself, describing the hum and how the pump behaves
      on a call is enough for us to have a strong idea what we're walking into before we arrive.
    </li>
  </ol>

  <h2>What each outcome typically means for cost</h2>
  <p>
    A start capacitor replacement is one of the least expensive common pool repairs — it's a
    small, inexpensive part and a quick swap. A seized shaft pointing to bearings or a seal is a
    larger job, and if the motor is older, that's often the point where a straight repair-vs-
    replace conversation makes sense. See our guide on{' '}
    <a href="/guides/pool-pump-repair-or-replace/">repair vs replacement for pool pumps</a> for
    how we think about that decision.
  </p>

  <h2>When to call a pro</h2>
  <p>
    Anything past the shaft-spin test — opening the motor housing, testing or replacing a
    capacitor, or working near the motor's electrical connections — is not a DIY repair.
    Capacitors can hold a charge even with power off, and motor work involves both electricity
    and water in close proximity. {SITE.feeLine}
  </p>

  <section class="faq">
    <h2>Common questions</h2>
    <h3>My pump hums but will not start. Is it dead?</h3>
    <p>
      Usually not. A hum with a free-spinning shaft points to a failed start capacitor, one of
      the cheapest and fastest pool repairs there is. It only means a dead motor when the shaft
      is seized.
    </p>
    <h3>Can a bad capacitor damage the motor if I leave it running (humming) too long?</h3>
    <p>
      Yes — a motor that hums under power without actually turning can overheat over time, since
      it's drawing current without doing mechanical work to dissipate it. Turning it off at the
      breaker until it's diagnosed is the safer move.
    </p>
    <h3>Is it worth replacing the motor instead of just the capacitor?</h3>
    <p>
      Not if the wet end and bearings are otherwise sound — a capacitor swap on an
      otherwise-healthy motor is a small, targeted fix. Replacement becomes the better
      conversation when the motor is old or the shaft-spin test points to bearings.
    </p>
    <h3>Does this apply to variable-speed pumps too?</h3>
    <p>
      Variable-speed motors are less commonly a straightforward capacitor issue and more often
      show drive fault codes instead — see our guide on{' '}
      <a href="/guides/variable-speed-pump-keeps-shutting-off/">why variable-speed pumps shut
      themselves off</a> if that matches what you're seeing.
    </p>
  </section>

  <Dispatch problem="Pump humming but not starting" area={SITE.serviceArea} />
</Base>
